    "created_at": "2016-05-26T13:40:31Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-05-26T13:40:55Z",
    "body": "@domenic \n\nProposed spec text:\n\n> For the purposes of the interaction of HTML with Selectors' :target pseudo-class, the target element is the indicated part of the document at the most recent run of the [\"scroll to the fragment identifier\" algorithm](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/browsers.html#scroll-to-the-fragment-identifier).\n\nGecko/Webkit/Blink follow the above text exactly. Details: \n- If an element is currently the styled target, DOM manipulation, _including changing the id/name of this element_, does not affect styling, nor does it affect the result of `querySelector()`\n- If the target is re-navigated to, the target element does not change.\n- There can only be one target\n\nThis doesn't match the current spec text due to the stickyness.\n\nTrident/Edge instead style every element with an id (not a name on an `<a>` tag!) matching the fragment.\n- When navigating, it will jump to the first such element in tree order as per the indicated element algorithm\n- All such elements match the `:target` selector both via styling and `querySelectorAll()`\n- Manipulating the dom will cause these elements to be restyled -- new elements with matching ids/names will also be styled, and if a currently styled element has its name/id changed, it will lose the styling accordingly\n- Navigating to a nonexistant fragment resets the styles\n\nThis doesn't match the current spec text since the current text calls for the first such element in tree order to be matched as the target, not all such elements.\n\nSpec text matching IE/Edge:\n\n> For the purposes of the interaction of HTML with Selectors' :target pseudo-class, the target element is all elements with an `id` matching the decoded fragid\n\n(using the definition of \"decoded fragid\" from the indicated part of the document algorithm)\n\nThis spec text matches fewer browsers than the proposed one. It also doesn't really follow the spirit of the `:target` selector, which seems to be used to highlight sections that the user has navigated to and to make pages like slideshows using CSS. I feel that the \"sticky\" proposed spec text is closer to what we want.\n",
    "author_association": "NONE",
